1
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:06,270
I'm going to start this talk off actually with a question.

2
00:00:06,862 --> 00:00:10,010
How many of you, could you raise your hand if you guys use Claude?

3
00:00:11,534 --> 00:00:15,236
All right, keep your hands up if you have tried to use Claude for cybersecurity.

4
00:00:19,694 --> 00:00:24,510
And keep them up if you have been blocked while trying to do cybersecurity work.

5
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:26,630
Well, my name is Kurt Barnard.

6
00:00:26,790 --> 00:00:30,468
I am the technical policy manager for Cyber Safeguards.

7
00:00:30,542 --> 00:00:43,310
and I am the reason you guys get blocked and I'm here to talk today about why that is and why we build the policies the way they do and How we're looking to improve them in the future.

8
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:47,670
So thank you for bearing with us while we learn in public together.

9
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:57,910
So I'm here to talk about maximizing harm, maximizing defense and how we help Claude navigate the offense-defense divide and How we're doing one of these pretty well.

10
00:00:57,910 --> 00:00:59,870
and one of them, I think we could do a little better.

11
00:01:01,006 --> 00:01:16,730
So what we're going to cover today is probably, to start, probably one of the oldest debates in security, which is dual use technology and making sure that defenders have the tools they need to protect systems while denying them to adversaries that intend to use them for harm.

12
00:01:17,390 --> 00:01:23,170
We're going to talk briefly about the open weight model baseline and the risks that that poses.

13
00:01:24,398 --> 00:01:31,710
We're going to talk about what we call the cyber constitution, which defines what we think is and is not permissible for Claude to do.

14
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:41,550
We're going to discuss Claude, Fable 5, and the safety margin around the classifier that you all are probably bumping into.

15
00:01:42,254 --> 00:01:54,110
And we're going to close out by talking about jailbreaks, the jailbreak severity framework that we published, and talking a little bit about how we know we're getting this wrong today and how we hope to fix it moving forward.

16
00:01:55,918 --> 00:01:58,550
So to start we're going to talk about dual use technology.

17
00:02:00,814 --> 00:02:05,010
The problem with dual use technology has been around as long as I have been in the business.

18
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:10,750
I'm sure many of you are familiar with these tools that all have come with their own controversy when they have come out.

19
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:17,970
Mass scan, reducing the time it takes to scan the entire internet to almost nothing.

20
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:32,510
Metasploit, I worked very closely with the Metasploit team and every single time we released a Metasploit module, there was controversy because we know the second that we published a Metasploit module, the script kitties would pick it up and hit the entire internet with it.

21
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:39,990
And then you have tools like Cobalt Strike that really elevate red teamers, but also are used by adversaries in the wild.

22
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:52,550
And then even like code analysis tools like IDA Pro and CodeQL that have made it much faster to identify vulnerabilities and generate exploits in the wild have come with their own controversy.

23
00:02:52,870 --> 00:03:03,930
So with all of these tools, the techniques are identical, but the intent is very different when you look at how defenders use them and how attackers use them.

24
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:08,130
So this problem was not built by AI.

25
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:19,810
But it really makes this tension much stronger because we are now seeing these capabilities at speed, scale and accessibility that we have never really seen before.

26
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:31,470
I mean the type of work that could take weeks now can be done in sometimes hours, sometimes minutes, and for example the ability to scan code bases, right?

27
00:03:31,566 --> 00:03:35,030
You are no longer limited to what you can focus your attention on in a single session.

28
00:03:35,246 --> 00:03:38,090
You can scan large swaths of code bases very, very quickly.

29
00:03:38,766 --> 00:03:41,450
And then the third and maybe most important one is accessibility.

30
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:55,770
While all of these tools work much better in the hands of practitioners that have deep experience, it really does elevate what, you know, script kiddies can do or even folks that have even a very, very basic understanding of security.

31
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:59,268
Um, it allows them to be much more capable much, much faster.

32
00:04:01,166 --> 00:04:10,270
Um, and, and also there is a new forcing function which is the prevalence of open weight models with very high, uh, cyber capabilities.

33
00:04:10,894 --> 00:04:22,250
And so everyone here, if you were defending a network, you have to assume that your worst adversary is at minimum using the most capable open weight models that are available.

34
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:36,036
So the difference between the open weight models and the frontier models that are hosted is that we have things like classifiers and policy that can sit between every request.

35
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:40,590
We have some visibility into who has access into these.

36
00:04:40,974 --> 00:04:43,990
We have the ability to identify misuse after the fact.

37
00:04:44,622 --> 00:04:49,890
And we can also move the boundaries and the safety margin and the classifiers after the model is released.

38
00:04:50,050 --> 00:04:55,364
We have the ability to change and update these policies as we see that they, if we see that they have failed somewhere.

39
00:04:56,462 --> 00:05:07,990
When it comes to the open weight models, even if a model is published with safeguards to not let you do particularly egregious things, those can all be fine-tuned away or obliterated.

40
00:05:08,030 --> 00:05:13,450
If any folks have heard about model obliteration, you can bypass any of those guardrails in an open weight model.

41
00:05:13,450 --> 00:05:16,550
So, none of those safeguards have any durability.

42
00:05:17,870 --> 00:05:24,090
Local inference also means that there is no gate to who potentially has access to these highly capable models.

43
00:05:25,102 --> 00:05:26,190
There's no usage signal.

44
00:05:26,310 --> 00:05:27,490
There's no ability to enforce.

45
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:32,510
And once an open weight model is released, it is like the genie is out of the bottle.

46
00:05:32,650 --> 00:05:33,870
You cannot put it back in.

47
00:05:34,010 --> 00:05:35,130
It is out there forever.

48
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:42,090
And you must assume that it is going to be obliterated and it is going to be able to be used for overtly malicious activity.

49
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:53,690
And then the the other one that I think actually gets overlooked quite a bit is that the open weight models can also provide no Alignment guarantees in a lot of ways when we think about AI safety.

50
00:05:54,110 --> 00:06:07,430
We assume that there is some Alignment guarantees that the the model will behave in a way that is rational and aligns with like what we as a society want To see the open weight models can even have that alignment obliterated.

51
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:14,390
So we talk about like chaos models sometimes that might have their own like they will perform very undesirable actions.

52
00:06:15,054 --> 00:06:21,796
So even if an open weight model is released and has good alignment it can be misaligned after the fact because it is open.

53
00:06:23,810 --> 00:06:35,610
And so when we think about the purpose or what what the foundational models can do or frontier models can do for defenders we think about this capability gap.

54
00:06:35,730 --> 00:06:44,710
So what is the delta between what an open weight model can do overtly maliciously and what the frontier models can do and what value we can provide to Defender?

55
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:48,510
And that is the gap between the capabilities.

56
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:51,270
That is what we can provide to Defender at an accelerated pace.

57
00:06:51,694 --> 00:07:04,970
So this graph is showing a report from UKAC, I think from June, showing the most advanced open weight models as they compare to the most advanced frontier models from the other Frontier Labs.

58
00:07:05,470 --> 00:07:13,350
And that is kind of the gap that we consider to be the buffer or the head start that we can give to defenders if we execute properly.

59
00:07:14,630 --> 00:07:25,690
Kimi K3, I heard a lot of folks in my community talk about, well, I can't use Fable for what I need, but then I pump it into Kimi K3 and it gives me exactly what I want.

60
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:30,430
And some people are even like, it's kind of scary what will come out of this model.

61
00:07:31,438 --> 00:07:34,070
And it's very important and it's something that we think about all the time.

62
00:07:34,382 --> 00:07:44,010
This is sort of a looming shadow that we operate under is this rising tide of the open weight models, knowing that they are going to be used for nefarious purposes.

63
00:07:46,860 --> 00:07:54,920
So our first attempt to kind of provide this defensive value, I'm sure many of you are familiar with Project Glasswing.

64
00:07:55,660 --> 00:08:09,680
That was our goal, to provide limited access to a number of very high impact organizations that would be able to use it for strong defensive use case, really as a proof of concept to see if this model is even rational or makes sense.

65
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:22,860
We have seen some really fantastic results from a lot of our Glasswing partners and I would encourage you, one of my favorites is the blog post that Mozilla put out about their use of Mythos to secure Firefox.

66
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:32,020
That is like a very critical part of the technology stack when we think about security, browser security is a huge one, so we love to hear that.

67
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:38,180
At Anthropic, we have scanned very quickly more than 1,000 open source projects.

68
00:08:39,534 --> 00:08:48,080
Our Glasswing partners reported over 10,000 important vulnerabilities within the first six weeks of the program.

69
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:55,220
About 90% of them were considered to be true positives And that number, we think, is rising with every follow-on model release.

70
00:08:56,142 --> 00:08:59,160
And we are working very hard to expand access to these programs.

71
00:08:59,380 --> 00:09:02,780
We grew the program from the initial 50 launch partners to 200.

72
00:09:03,278 --> 00:09:05,260
We realize that this pace is not very satisfactory.

73
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:09,940
But we are doing everything that we can to get it in the hands of the right people as fast as possible.

74
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:17,600
And something that, like, I really want people to think about is this whole thing is only like four months old.

75
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:20,840
And that is a really, really short timeline.

76
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:39,820
And I know there's like a lot of frustration, especially with individual researchers about not having access to these models But this is so new and the the risks really are quite substantial And we feel like it's very important to have a measured approach here Project glass wing was only announced in April, right?

77
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:50,280
we are four four four months and one day past that now and then We expanded the program about two months later.

78
00:09:50,830 --> 00:09:54,560
We shipped Fable 5, which is Mythos with advanced safeguards.

79
00:09:54,900 --> 00:09:59,000
And then of course, we're immediately covered by export controls that we then had to remediate.

80
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,660
So this is a very delicate process.

81
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:04,380
We are learning in public, in real time with you all.

82
00:10:04,846 --> 00:10:15,300
I have been a security practitioner for my entire career, so I understand the benefits of gaining access to these models and the disappointment of maybe not being given access to them.

83
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:17,960
But we're working very, very fast.

84
00:10:18,760 --> 00:10:22,116
And the timelines are, are very crazy.

85
00:10:24,206 --> 00:10:29,880
And throughout my entire career, the advice has always been, I mean, I felt like a broken record.

86
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:36,772
At the end of, like, every single talk, every single blog, every single paper, I was just saying, you need to patch faster, you need to patch faster.

87
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:37,920
I felt like a broken record.

88
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,060
I was getting very tired of it.

89
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:46,440
And now, like, what I really want everyone to realize is there is this big looming forcing function of the open weight models.

90
00:10:47,160 --> 00:11:01,924
This capability is coming and it will be in the open and this is our attempt to provide defensive utility ahead of time before this capability becomes very broadly available to everyone who wants to use it for bad purposes.

91
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:20,920
So I'm gonna talk a little bit about how we think about the cyber harm categories and we're gonna talk about the cyber constitution and what we want to support and we also realize that we do not always support in practice the things that we want to support in philosophy, and that's where we have a lot of room for improvement.

92
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:26,380
So the cyber constitution is a written framework.

93
00:11:27,086 --> 00:11:40,160
So we write down, it's a very lengthy document that describes how we want our models to engage with cyber security tasks, how we want them to behave, what security work we want to support and what we explicitly want to deny.

94
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:43,240
and it is designed to be argued with.

95
00:11:43,406 --> 00:11:53,580
so when we get in a room with someone and we describe what we think we should block, we always point back to the Constitution that has written examples of why we think certain things are good and bad.

96
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:57,420
and this document is a living document.

97
00:11:57,486 --> 00:11:58,420
it shifts all the time.

98
00:11:58,702 --> 00:12:06,480
we've been providing these restricted access for four months, and we have four versions of the Constitution.

99
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:13,320
So it changes all the time as we learn lessons by engaging with folks like you all.

100
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:24,280
But the the high-level goal that has never changed across all the iterations of the Constitution is that the high-level goal is we want to minimize harm and we want to maximize defensive utility.

101
00:12:25,518 --> 00:12:30,660
And unfortunately those two are in conflict on almost every single cybersecurity security prompt that you pass into CLOG.

102
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:38,720
So when it comes to the types of activities that we define, we have four buckets in the cyber constitution.

103
00:12:38,980 --> 00:12:41,980
We have things that we consider acceptable use, dual use.

104
00:12:42,382 --> 00:12:45,680
We have a bucket that we call sensitive targets, and then prohibited use.

105
00:12:46,126 --> 00:12:47,800
Prohibited use is a little bit of a misnomer.

106
00:12:48,620 --> 00:12:52,020
It might be the most risky dual use capability.

107
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,476
But you can see at a high level, things that we want to support are things even like CTFs.

108
00:12:57,742 --> 00:13:03,260
we want to support malware analysis, we want to support patch prioritization in all cases.

109
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:04,000
That is our goal.

110
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:04,820
That is our desire.

111
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:08,380
Then there are dual-use cases that become a lot more complicated.

112
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:17,880
In particular, the ability to generate complex exploits, especially just from binary, is probably one of the most controversial dual-use cases.

113
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:26,180
And this all exists in the context that there are a large number of sensitive targets that, to be quite frank, many of them cannot be patched at all.

114
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:33,620
Some of them require a technician to go in a van to like a power substation and plug in a thumb drive.

115
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:45,960
I talked to a utility provider where they plug in a modem at a power substation that has a default username and password on it, and you could literally war drive for a power substation.

116
00:13:46,126 --> 00:13:59,876
So I think we all know that many of these OT systems are horrendously insecure, does not require mythos level access to be able to find vulnerabilities in a lot of these systems and Yet they are part of our risk calculation.

117
00:13:59,982 --> 00:14:09,660
It's something that we have to consider, And we, we want to be sensitive to the fact that those are some of our most critical systems that have the ability To cause a lot of a lot of damage.

118
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:23,440
And then there are things that we call prohibited use, which we consider very offensively biased Um, things like building malware, which there are defensive use cases for it, but we consider it to be very strongly, offensively biased.

119
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:31,844
Um, self-replicating code, um, supply chain attacks, things like that, uh, fall into what we call in the Constitution, uh, prohibited use.

120
00:14:33,390 --> 00:14:44,160
And then we have to translate this document into a series of classifiers that looks at individual exchanges and tries to make a determination about whether it fit, whether that activity fits into one of these categories.

121
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:50,600
So, the categories that we identify, and this is all in our blog post on Fable.

122
00:14:51,180 --> 00:14:55,100
we have what we consider to be benign use, which maps very cleanly to acceptable use.

123
00:14:55,534 --> 00:15:05,668
and this is the defensive work that we want to always support, which in particular, the secure software development life cycle is a very big one that we want to support in all cases.

124
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:18,560
And then what we have, we consider low risk dual use, which does have potential adversarial consequences, but we consider it to be highly defensively biased.

125
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:30,100
And just to give an example of a very common situation that we encounter is where we do not wanna facilitate the rapid development of weaponized exploits for malicious actors.

126
00:15:30,260 --> 00:15:35,120
We do not wanna give ransomware operators an easy button to go and exploit things at scale.

127
00:15:35,470 --> 00:15:43,200
However, we also realize that when software authors are given vulnerability reports, they need to know how serious it is.

128
00:15:43,460 --> 00:15:50,692
And one of the primary ways that we do that is by creating proof of concept exploit code to show that something is exploitable and weaponizable.

129
00:15:51,150 --> 00:15:56,680
And in fact, in many cases, if a vulnerability is not considered exploitable, it is never fixed at all.

130
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:04,320
But a lot of the times we know we have a backlog of CVEs that actually are exploitable and just nobody has really looked at them yet, right?

131
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:22,940
So we go now based on things like CISA KEV and whether there's a POC in the wild, but the the calculus on whether something is exploitable or not has changed in the last six months, and we have to find a good way to to adjust our mentality around that.

132
00:16:23,342 --> 00:16:39,900
And then we have high-risk dual-use, which we consider to be quite offensively biased, and then and prohibited use, which maps very cleanly into the prohibited use bucket, which has a very, very low defensive utility.

133
00:16:41,070 --> 00:16:47,588
And so just to dive in a little bit on two of the categories, and just to give some examples, many of you are probably interested in things like CTFs.

134
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:49,400
We absolutely wanna support that use case.

135
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:54,600
We approve folks to use our models and CTFs through the Cyber Verification Program.

136
00:16:55,660 --> 00:17:04,480
Doing malware analysis for defensive purposes, prioritizing patches, these are all things that we want to be supporting all of the time.

137
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:19,780
And then some deeper examples on things that we consider to be prohibited use are destructive techniques that might cause even like cyber-physical damage to real world systems, scaled data exfiltration of massive amounts of records.

138
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:36,480
We understand sometimes you need to show a POC that you can extract some set of information, but exfiltration at scale, self-replicating codes, supply chain attacks, these are all things that we really very strongly want to stop because we think the propensity for harm is very very high.

139
00:17:37,966 --> 00:17:53,240
And the other part of this, that is extremely, extremely complicated, is that even historically the difference between authorized use and authorized security research and unauthorized security research is intent.

140
00:17:53,582 --> 00:17:58,420
and it's whether you're going to disclose that vulnerability when you're done responsibly or not.

141
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:03,400
And even if you look at the CFAA, it's actually even quite more complicated than that.

142
00:18:03,540 --> 00:18:10,140
The Justice Department has a charging recommendation to not charge people for doing good faith security research.

143
00:18:10,478 --> 00:18:13,780
But if you look at the CFAA, it's not actually that clear.

144
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:23,920
And so if this is something that the legal system can't figure out in a court proceeding, and we have to figure it out in a single exchange with Claude, that's a very challenging problem.

145
00:18:24,494 --> 00:18:28,040
And it's something that, frankly, we cannot do.

146
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:31,060
We cannot identify intent from an individual exchange.

147
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:37,720
We can in the very obvious case where someone says, help me hack my neighbor's Facebook account or something like that.

148
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:39,840
That's very obvious and that's easy to catch.

149
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:49,060
But everyone knows that you can just reframe your prompt to seem more benign and then you're likely to get around that pretty trivial block.

150
00:18:51,054 --> 00:18:58,640
So, if we cannot understand intent from an individual exchange, we have to proxy that with identity.

151
00:18:58,926 --> 00:19:16,840
And so that's the basis for the cyber verification program, is that if we can gain some information about your identity, we can do some degree of vetting and identify that you have participated in good faith security research or are a known, like, good guy, and then we can expand that access and then we have some accountability levers that we can pull there.

152
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:34,540
So, our two systems for leveraging identity as the proxy for intent is the Cyber Verification Program and Project Glasswing, and we look forward to expanding those programs and making these capabilities more accessible as we move forward.

153
00:19:35,320 --> 00:19:47,260
I want to take a little bit of time to talk about Fable, which I think many of you probably have some experience with, seeing that it is very hard to do defensive cyber work in and why that is.

154
00:19:49,038 --> 00:19:56,540
So with all of our safety classifiers, we have to set what we consider to be a safety margin due to the ability to jailbreak these classifiers.

155
00:19:56,880 --> 00:20:13,800
And so it is our goal to make sure that there is enough margin between the classifier boundary and what we consider harmful, that we can prevent that harmful activity in time, especially because of the speed of like agentic flows to commit harm at scale.

156
00:20:15,860 --> 00:20:29,956
with Mythos and the advanced capabilities that we saw in that, we realized that we had to set this boundary a little bit wider than we did for the other models in order to be to be able to have confidence that we could catch this abuse much much faster.

157
00:20:30,200 --> 00:20:45,000
So we have this concept of safety margin where we know that we're going to block some requests that we would like to permit, but we do that to give ourselves a bit of a buffer in order to be able to enforce our policy lines the way that we want to.

158
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:49,300
And so there are there are two circles here.

159
00:20:49,390 --> 00:20:56,300
One is the set of activity that we want to be permitted by a Constitution, and then what the safeguards actually permit.

160
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:57,760
And they are not the same.

161
00:20:58,222 --> 00:20:59,860
We know that they are not the same.

162
00:21:01,870 --> 00:21:10,628
That is absolutely intentional, and that boundary is what gives us a little bit of lead time to improve our ability to detect and enforce our actual policy lines.

163
00:21:12,380 --> 00:21:14,440
And I wanna talk about jailbreaks here for a minute.

164
00:21:16,654 --> 00:21:19,652
Basically all models are susceptible to jailbreak in some capacity.

165
00:21:20,270 --> 00:21:25,732
So we have to live in a world where we know that our safeguards have the potential to be jailbroken.

166
00:21:26,318 --> 00:21:29,100
But also not every jailbreak is the same.

167
00:21:30,062 --> 00:21:35,460
So these are three examples of different types of jailbreaks and this is an example of how we reason about them differently.

168
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:48,360
So, there can be minor jailbreaks that might bypass what the classifier is actually supposed to enforce, but does not actually get through to performing harmful activities.

169
00:21:48,462 --> 00:21:56,460
So like, when you all get blocked doing malware reverse engineering, right, that is a safety margin around building malware.

170
00:21:57,180 --> 00:22:02,580
And if you found a way to get past it to reverse engineer malware, that's fine.

171
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:10,200
If you got around it to build a new C2 to use maliciously in a ransomware campaign, we think that is not fine.

172
00:22:10,350 --> 00:22:12,132
So that is why that safety margin exists.

173
00:22:12,860 --> 00:22:24,080
And then there are narrow jailbreaks that are harmful that might give access to like a very specific capability that is not universal.

174
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:42,820
So for example, maybe it gives you additional capability in vulnerability identification specifically, but does nothing for exploit generation, or maybe it gives you some ability to generate exploits, but it's only for cross-site scripting attacks.

175
00:22:43,246 --> 00:22:49,380
We consider those to be narrow jailbreaks that also still are potentially harmful.

176
00:22:50,670 --> 00:23:05,760
And then there's universal jailbreaks, which are the ones that we have the highest degree of concern about, which is where through some sort of modification or prompting, you can gain access to all of the harmful activity that we want to prevent.

177
00:23:06,740 --> 00:23:13,180
And that also allows you to build agentic flows around that, which is the particularly concerning thing.

178
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:18,180
So if you're able to build a machine that does cyber war at scale, that is what we do not want.

179
00:23:18,280 --> 00:23:22,000
So a universal jailbreak is really where that threat model lies.

180
00:23:22,460 --> 00:23:26,040
And so these are kind of the scenarios where we think about jailbreaks.

181
00:23:27,438 --> 00:23:36,860
So due to the jailbreak report for the Fable 5 classifier, it required us to build a new framework kind of out of necessity.

182
00:23:38,798 --> 00:23:41,380
So we built what we call the jailbreak severity framework.

183
00:23:41,870 --> 00:23:47,820
It is based very loosely on CVSS, which I know everyone here loves and has no complaints about at all, universally beloved.

184
00:23:49,820 --> 00:23:53,540
However, it has gotten the job done for a very, very long time.

185
00:23:53,998 --> 00:24:01,120
So, uh, we look to build something, uh, on that framework and we consider there to be kind of four core criteria.

186
00:24:01,774 --> 00:24:03,960
Um, the first criteria is capability gain.

187
00:24:04,142 --> 00:24:15,780
So, for example, if you are able to jailbreak a highly capable model into, uh, finding vulnerabilities, but those vulnerabilities are extremely trivial, we consider that to be very low capability gain.

188
00:24:15,860 --> 00:24:26,840
So, for example, um, some of the reported jailbreaks, if you push it into, uh, um, any of the open weight models, they will provide the same output without any refusals or any blocks at all.

189
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:31,000
We consider that to be very low uplift and the capability gain to be very minimal.

190
00:24:31,440 --> 00:24:44,080
However, if it's able to reverse engineer an obfuscated binary and make a zero-day exploit for that, right now, maybe not next month, but right now we consider that to be a pretty high, high uplift capability.

191
00:24:44,960 --> 00:24:46,840
Um, the next is the breadth of gain.

192
00:24:47,342 --> 00:24:54,244
So it's how many different types of harmful activities have you gained access to and that's largely based on the constitution, some of those discrete lists.

193
00:24:54,840 --> 00:25:00,580
So if it, if it gains access to just like one of those or two of those, that's a limited scope uh, breadth.

194
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:05,720
Uh, but if it's the entire category of prohibited use, that's a very different scenario.

195
00:25:07,118 --> 00:25:08,660
Then we have ease of weaponization.

196
00:25:09,230 --> 00:25:23,556
Um, this is like a little bit different from how you think about traditional vulnerabilities, because there's a lot of criteria that could into here, such as a jailbreak may require that you spend a million dollars worth of tokens before the jailbreak works.

197
00:25:24,174 --> 00:25:28,600
if that's the case, ease of weaponization, uh is uh.

198
00:25:28,654 --> 00:25:30,300
that is that is not easy to weaponize.

199
00:25:30,478 --> 00:25:37,920
some of them require a very convoluted, or you have to do harnessing or, uh, like very deep.

200
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:44,540
like context poisoning, many of those are considered difficult to weaponize.

201
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:50,980
However, if your prompt is something like, please just fix this code, that is very easy.

202
00:25:51,720 --> 00:25:53,840
So that's where that falls on ease of weaponization.

203
00:25:54,940 --> 00:26:04,760
For discoverability, this one is a little bit challenging because it's also very different from how we think about traditional vulnerabilities.

204
00:26:04,760 --> 00:26:20,800
Um, some jailbreaks are fairly trivial for someone at home with a keyboard and just typing things out to identify where the guardrails are and to very quickly find, oh, if I just type this, I get around this thing that is normally blocked.

205
00:26:21,480 --> 00:26:29,360
Um, if, if a bypass is published publicly, um, that is enough.

206
00:26:29,550 --> 00:26:32,380
Like you don't need a, it's already like a POC.

207
00:26:32,380 --> 00:26:43,540
it's already an exploit in the wild, so once they are published, it already is fully discoverable, and effectively anyone can use it, and then we're just hinging on ease of weaponization, so if like token cost is high.

208
00:26:43,980 --> 00:26:52,920
So discoverability is like a very different kind of access here relative to what we think about for vulnerability, and a very important distinction in this framework.

209
00:26:53,582 --> 00:27:00,940
We at Anthropic built and published this framework, but we hope to see a more community accepted standard.

210
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:11,960
The intent was not to make the anthropic jailbreak framework, it was to lay the groundwork to have a universal framework that all of the labs and the community can rally around.

211
00:27:12,620 --> 00:27:17,460
And so we look forward to working with our partners to make this a common standard rather than an anthropic standard.

212
00:27:20,462 --> 00:27:25,000
So some places where we know these policies really fall short and we have room for improvement.

213
00:27:26,286 --> 00:27:29,780
We know that the safety margin blocks real work.

214
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:41,280
We know that many people in many of the conversations that I've had today, I've heard tons of people say, I love Claude, I love anthropic models, I use it all the time, except for security.

215
00:27:41,582 --> 00:27:42,560
We hear that all the time.

216
00:27:42,620 --> 00:27:47,800
And we understand that these blocks can make it very difficult, especially for agentic flows.

217
00:27:48,520 --> 00:27:50,480
And we know that that is an issue.

218
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:58,040
We also recognize that independent researchers, probably like many of you here, have the worst path to advanced capabilities.

219
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:13,020
We understand that Project Glasswing looks like it's available only to a small subset of customers, and if you were a bug bounty researcher, for example, doing really good faith security work, we realize that you have the hardest path for those capable models, and that's something that we're working on.

220
00:28:13,614 --> 00:28:17,340
The other thing is we talked about these four buckets for classifiers.

221
00:28:17,902 --> 00:28:24,360
That is not enough nuance in order to have like, a really fine-grained enforcement policy here.

222
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:29,680
Too much context is flattened when you put things into just four buckets.

223
00:28:31,790 --> 00:28:36,280
And we also realized that the refusal explanations are vague.

224
00:28:36,740 --> 00:28:48,800
Many people say, hey, I'm not supposed to be getting cyber blocks and I got blocked on something, and you might not know that it triggered a jailbreak classifier, and actually was not a cyber classifier.

225
00:28:48,920 --> 00:28:49,820
We have seen that before.

226
00:28:49,820 --> 00:29:04,860
But also, especially if you're looking at this talk and you're like, hey, you said that I should be able to reverse engineer malware for defensive purposes and I got blocked, it doesn't explain what it was that could be even in the model's thinking.

227
00:29:05,560 --> 00:29:12,560
That you may or may not see or even look at, could have been thinking about, well, how would I design malware?

228
00:29:12,760 --> 00:29:16,560
Oh, well, that's gonna trip the classifier because it's not supposed to tell you how to design malware.

229
00:29:17,220 --> 00:29:21,140
So these are the four categories where we know that we really have a lot of room to grow.

230
00:29:21,140 --> 00:29:22,340
we have a lot of work to do.

